 read: '<strong>Zero OSHA recordables, zero lost-time injuries, zero restricted-work cases and zero fatalities across 165,987 man-hours \u2014 TRIR, DART and LTIFR all at 0.00.</strong> <strong>The lagging record is clean; the leading indicators are where the attention belongs.</strong> July logged 39 safety events, the second-highest month of the year, and 38 of the 39 are violations: 24 unsafe conditions and 14 unsafe behaviours. The monthly trend runs 0, 1, 17, 13, 45, 31, 39. <strong>Two leading lines read zero: safety observation rounds with project management in every month of 2026, and the safety client advisor visit in July after one in each of March, April, May and June.</strong> <strong>The July environmental record carries the item with the longest reach: the 15 July frac-out spread drilling fluid over an extensive area despite the bore depth having been increased from design, and the conclusion recorded at the time was that greater depth alone would not prevent it. Further events followed on 19 and 27 August. That finding is our own, and it is consistent with the drilling subcontractor\u2019s position of 27 August \u2014 worth weighing before the MV overrun is treated as a performance failure.</strong>'
 + '<strong>The exposure is in the leading indicators, not the lagging ones. July logged 39 safety events \u2014 the second-highest month of the year \u2014 and 38 of the 39 are violations: 24 unsafe conditions and 14 unsafe behaviours. The trend runs 0, 1, 17, 13, 45, 31, 39.</strong> '
 + '<strong>Two governance lines read ZERO and matter more than any of the above: Safety Observation Rounds with Project Management have been ZERO in every month of 2026, and the safety client advisor visit dropped to zero in July after one in each of March, April, May and June.</strong> '
 + '<strong>The July environmental record is the item that reaches furthest: the 15 July frac-out was already the second, and GreenSol concluded then that greater bore depth alone would not stop it. Further events followed on 19 August and 27 August. ITS\u2019s position of 27 August \u2014 that these conditions cannot be eliminated by its means and methods \u2014 is therefore consistent with our own July finding, which strengthens ITS\u2019s schedule argument and should be weighed before the MV overrun is pressed as a performance failure.</strong>'
